A||||7||Pretender||egr tube?||||5||
Z||000000||Pretender||04-30-2000||10:25 PM||||the egr tube coming from the valve itself bolts up to the upper intake? or does it just slide in? when i installed the new y-pipe i think it broke? i have it just butting up to the intake where it looks like it would go. My question is do i need to buy a new egr tube and bolt it up to the intake? is this the part that is expensive and very fragle? <P>the car is an 88gt.<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||Old Lar||04-30-2000||11:02 PM||||Of course it bolts to the intake and to the EGR valve.  Two bolts each side.  You will have an enjotable time on the intake side. The cost is relative.  Just bought one from Mr Goodwrench (they are still in stock) for $65.03.  I got a discount cause I give them a lot of business(or is it I get the business). I help clean out old inventory.  The estimate for them to do the replacement was 3 hrs @$56/hr.<P>I haven't used it, EGR pipe wasn't the problem.  It goes into my parts inventory.<P>Part #10137917 pipe kit.<P>FOCOA has one that supposedly will never wear out for $79 (I think).  Fiero Store has them.  I don't think they are fragile, but get corroded and break.  When they break you car runs like crap.||||reg||6
Z||000002||Fformula88||04-30-2000||11:40 PM||||I've had it off a couple of times... including putting in a new one.  It isn't too hard of a job if you have a little patience.  I cannot believe they quoted you three hours of labor to do the job!!!  My mere amatuer removal and replacement couldn't have been over a half hour!  One hint... take off the distributor cap, it gives you a little more clearance to get a wrench to the intake plenum bolts.  Good Luck!||||reg||1
Z||000003||Pretender||04-30-2000||11:49 PM||||So this could be the reason why my car seems to idle high? Thats kinda expensive for a little tube. Thats just one more new part, pretty soon i'm going to have a brand new '88 Gt with 90,000 miles. thanks for anwsering my question guys..||||reg||5
Z||000004||prof bobo||05-01-2000||03:09 PM||||1. Use the I.M. end of old tube as a template to make a cover for the hole in the I.M. from a small piece of metal.<P>2.  Use a couple of pieces of shim stock between the EGR valve and its mount to block the EGR passage.<P>3. Throw the tube away.<P>Cost, $0.  Or so.<P>Works fine 150,000 miles later on an engine that already had 100,000+ on it.||||reg||12
Z||000005||Pretender||05-02-2000||01:57 AM||||And how often does your check engine light come on? No offence, but i'm going to fix it the right way. Not that your way is the wrong way. It's just not my way. L8r and thanks for the input.||||reg||10
Z||000006||fieroshop||05-02-2000||10:59 AM||||Boy do I love to see you guys sweat and cuss because you do thing the hard way. I replace egr tubes all the time and from start to finish it takes me a whole 20 min or so. I just undo the two bolts that hold the egr tube to the egr valve and using a 10mm 12 point socket I undo all the bolts on the plenum and undo the map sensor and very carefully pull out the plenum and the egr tube out as a unit. Make sure that you undo the vacumm hose under the plenum. Next I replace the egr tube and reassemble evrything. I charg people a half hour and with an hourly rate of $60.00 they pay $30.00<BR>But if you guys want to groan and moan and cuss then go ahead and do it your way. Me I'm too old for back breaking, bending over the engine type of work for long periods. Oh I forgot most of you guys are still very young just barely out of the crib so I guess using Brawn instead of brain works better.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Pretender||05-02-2000||02:33 PM||||Who said anything about doing this the hard way and cussing about it??? I'm not goiong to take off my intake for the two bolts that i can reach after i move the dist. cap. I don't mind reaching and bending. Guess it's because i'm barely out of the cradle and can do that sort of stuff without taking motrin or bayer asprin.||||reg||5
